WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.080
Context helps the model understand the situation.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:12.820
Useful context may include domain background, definitions, source text, user goals, assumptions, available tools, previous decisions, or known limitations.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.800
In a document review workflow, the context might include the document excerpt, the review goal, and the audience.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:25.820
In a software workflow, it might include the API contract, error logs, coding standard, and expected behavior.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.360
Constraints narrow the acceptable output.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:40.420
They may describe length, tone, reading level, required terminology, allowed sources, prohibited content, output sections, uncertainty language, or citation expectations.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.280
Constraints are helpful when they reflect real requirements.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.100
They become harmful when they contradict each other, overload the prompt, or include irrelevant detail.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.720
More instructions are not automatically better.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.580
A practical prompt structure is easy to scan.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.240
Start with the task.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.700
Add context or source material.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.280
State constraints.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.500
Specify the output format.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.400
Add quality checks.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:12.380
A role or operating perspective can be useful when it guides vocabulary or viewpoint, such as "write for a support operations manager."

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.040
But a stated role does not create real expertise, authority, access, or credentials.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.780
The application still needs controls for permissions and actions.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.880
Instruction priority also depends on architecture.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:32.460
System, developer, user, tool, and retrieved instructions may have different priority in a deployed application.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:40.520
Retrieved files, webpages, and tool output should be treated as data unless the application explicitly trusts them as instructions.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:48.660
Good prompt structure separates trusted operating instructions from untrusted content, making conflicts easier to identify and safer to handle.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.960
Organization is part of reliability.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.860
Put related requirements together instead of scattering them across the prompt.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.920
If format rules matter, place them under an output format section.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:02.500
If refusal or escalation rules matter, name them clearly.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:07.600
If source text is untrusted, label it as source material, not as operating instructions.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:12.460
This structure helps humans review the prompt and helps the model follow the intended hierarchy.
